    "content": "Vaccinations are vital health tools that teach the body's immune system to recognize and fight harmful viruses and bacteria, preventing over 30 dangerous infections. They protect against diseases like measles, polio, COVID-19, and flu, saving millions of lives annually. Vaccines are available for all ages through doctors and pharmacies. Key Vaccine Information Routine Adult Vaccinations: Recommended vaccines for adults (19–64) include COVID-19, Tdap (Tetanus, Diphtheria, Pertussis), and Hepatitis A/B [2, CDC's website 7]. Preventive Benefits: Vaccines protect both individuals and society by reducing infection rates, such as the HPV vaccine reducing cancer risks. Childhood Immunizations: Children are recommended to receive vaccinations for measles, mumps, rubella (MMR), polio, and meningitis [1, HHS.gov 9]. Availability: Pharmacies like Walgreens and local health departments (e.g., Virginia) offer, in many cases, walk-in services. Types of Vaccine-Preventable Diseases Respiratory: COVID-19, Flu (Influenza), RSV. Bacterial/Infection: Meningitis (MenACWY/MenB), Pneumococcal, Tetanus. Viral: HPV, Hepatitis B, Measles, Mumps, Rubella, Chickenpox. Vaccination Safety & Effectiveness Vaccines work with your body’s natural defenses to build protection. They are highly effective at preventing severe illness and death. Vaccination prevents 3.5 to 5 million deaths every year.",
